> []($block.attrs('hi'))
>
> I curate here a technical [flight manual](/flight-manual/) that primarily
> means to offer practical—albeit perhaps overwhelming comprehensive—*flight
> rules* towards mastering your software development craft.
>
> I am fairly slow and deliberate.  
> I intend to keep putting out articles at the approximate rate of one per week,
> but my propensity for ranting tangents significantly hinders the production of
> articles getting to the point at all, let alone straight.  
> If you stick around a short while, I'm sure you'll come to find the irony in
> that.
>
> You'll find some meta information regarding the technology stack that this Web
> site is built upon in its [colophon](/colophon/).

# [User guide]($section.id('user-guide'))

This establishment hosts three families of writing.  The [flight
manual](/flight-manual/) is the grand assemblage; its individual articles are
the *flight rules* proper, namely the practical, fairly thorough guides one may
consult with actual intent to improve one's craft.

[Flashcards](/flashcards/) are their shorter, more digestible cousins, each
chiefly concerned with lodging one useful notion, pitfall or habit into memory.
[Ramblings](/ramblings/), meanwhile, are where I permit myself greater altitude
and less immediate utility, in pursuit of perspective, cautionary tales, or the
occasional dignified grumble.

This Web site's colour scheme automatically adjusts to your preference as
indicated by your Web browser, which should forward that of your desktop
environment; though it currently looks better in **light** mode.

Web browsers supporting [View
Transitions](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/View_Transition_API)
will animate, in a non-nauseating manner, various anchor points as you peruse
the site. This functionality may be disabled by indicating, through your browser
again, your preference for reduced motion.

Links like [this one](/404/) point to other places on this very blog; external
references such as [that
one](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Attributes/rel#external)
are annotated with an arrow pointing up and to the right.

For the curious ravenous, the section labelled [*Flight Rules*](/flight-manual/)
contains the main course. The articles there result from a great deal of time,
effort, and *learned experience*, and [are generally nothing alike those you
would find elsewhere
online](https://ccjmne.sh/blog/ramblings/on-shoddy-pseudo-tech-guides/): mine
are drenched, coated and laced in some uniquely overwhelming insight into the
greater ecosystem as a whole, while also not shying away from delving to the
very bottom of any technical well.

For the curious curious, [Flashcards](/flashcards/) are their shorter, more
focused, digestible cousins, each chiefly concerned with incepting some notion
into the curious minds.

For the curious contemplative, finally, I also share some
[ramblings](/ramblings/) that are more conceptual or less grounded in relevance
and practicality, but may be consumed more recreationally—or precautionarily.
